WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.127
Now we load the sample knowledge base into a Pandas DataFrame.

00:00:04.127 --> 00:00:08.752
The code is intentionally simple.

00:00:08.752 --> 00:00:15.577
We read the CSV, check its shape, and inspect a few columns such as document ID, title, topic, and source.

00:00:15.577 --> 00:00:22.627
At this point, I want you to develop a habit: inspect the raw data before transforming it.

00:00:22.627 --> 00:00:27.202
Confirm how many documents you have.

00:00:27.202 --> 00:00:32.081
Check the column names. Look for missing values or unexpected text.

00:00:32.693 --> 00:00:36.335
The sample dataset contains ten source documents.

00:00:36.335 --> 00:00:45.070
Later, those ten documents will become more than ten rows because long documents can be split into multiple chunks.

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:52.639
This distinction matters. A source document and a retrieval chunk are not necessarily the same thing.

00:00:52.639 --> 00:00:54.304
For now, keep the original DataFrame intact.

00:00:54.304 --> 00:01:01.508
We will create a separate chunk table rather than overwriting the source collection.
